const R=[
 {k:'f1',ic:'🍰',c:['Fractions. One group aims to learn how to solve the problems, the','other simply to solve them; in each group, half the students judge','their own capabilities after every session.'],q:'Which combination did worse than the other three?',o:[['Performance goal without','self-evaluation'],['Learning goal without','self-evaluation'],['Performance goal with','self-evaluation']],say:`<b>Performance goal without self-evaluation.</b> Both learning-goal groups and the performance-goal group that evaluated itself did better on self-efficacy, skill and motivation (Schunk, 1996).`},
 {k:'f2',ic:'🔁',c:['Putting both of Schunk\'s (1996) studies together.'],q:'When students self-evaluate often, does the type of goal still matter?',o:[['Hardly: frequent self-evaluation','helped with either goal'],['Yes: only learning','goals work'],['Yes: only performance','goals work']],say:`<b>Frequent self-evaluation</b> raised achievement outcomes regardless of goal type; infrequent self-evaluation promoted self-regulated learning and self-efficacy only with learning goals. Self-evaluation may fit better with learning goals than with performance goals. Schunk and Ertmer (1999) replicated this with college students learning computer skills.`},
 {k:'f3',ic:'📒',c:['Fifth- and sixth-graders in mathematics either record now and then','whether they are working on the right materials, set daily','performance goals, or do both.'],q:'What raised time on task and achievement?',o:[['Self-monitoring'],['Setting daily','goals'],['Neither of','the two']],say:`<b>Self-monitoring</b> significantly increased time on task and mathematical achievement; goal setting had minimal effects (Sagotsky, Patterson &amp; Lepper, 1978).`},
 {k:'f4',ic:'🤔',c:['Goal setting did little in that study.'],q:'What explanation did the authors suggest?',o:[['The children may have needed training','in setting challenging, attainable goals'],['Goals never work','for children']],say:`<b>Training in goal setting.</b> The authors thought the children might first have needed to learn how to set goals that are hard but reachable. Compare the goal lab: the properties of a goal matter.`},
 {k:'f5',ic:'🧑‍🏫',c:['Children learning mathematics: some record their own progress after','each session, for others an adult records it, others are not monitored.'],q:'What happened to self-efficacy and achievement?',o:[['Self- and adult monitoring equally','good, both better than none'],['Only self-monitoring','helped'],['Only adult','monitoring helped']],say:`<b>Equally good</b> (Schunk, 1983b). Self-monitoring and external monitoring enhanced self-efficacy and achievement equally well and better than no monitoring.`},
 {k:'f6',ic:'🔑',c:['The three groups completed the same amount of work during practice.'],q:'So what was the key to the effect?',o:[['That progress was monitored,','not who monitored it'],['How much work','they completed'],['Who did the','monitoring']],say:`<b>Monitoring of progress.</b> Effects did not depend on session performance, because the groups did not differ in work completed. The key was monitoring of progress rather than who performed it.`}];
